" :step/next "step-2a22f052-de27-5830-aad8-c51679a4477f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2a22f052-de27-5830-aad8-c51679a4477f" :step/id #uuid "2a22f052-de27-5830-aad8-c51679a4477f" :step/content "question-b1b90563-ce29-5141-8ac6-0ac20bb77c12" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166928 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e335f33f-c98e-5cab-bfc9-af13ddc0ea7b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e335f33f-c98e-5cab-bfc9-af13ddc0ea7b" :question/id #uuid "e335f33f-c98e-5cab-bfc9-af13ddc0ea7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-334872" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-2y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\n$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.5,\\,1]^{T},$ we get\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"40px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,.$</td>\n</tr><tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-xy)=2-4x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-4x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-4(0.5)-(1) & -(0.5) \\\\ 4(1) & -2+4(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3f54b2e5-b5e2-51e5-8a3a-e2a8b88f4fac" "answer-field-762648f5-1207-5f3e-be39-97bd8eb92965" "answer-field-0d254612-5df9-5924-b8e3-9685b0af8029" "answer-field-95323910-156c-5adf-a3a0-32d0fac9f9e0" "answer-field-36aad4f6-443d-53e2-8ee7-dee965880354" "answer-field-3ccf70ea-36cd-52da-a930-ff1480d7052b" "answer-field-536d2a25-1a52-5d05-808f-819be5037ebc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3f54b2e5-b5e2-51e5-8a3a-e2a8b88f4fac" :answer-field/id #uuid "3f54b2e5-b5e2-51e5-8a3a-e2a8b88f4fac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-69a8ea1e-5e95-555b-b649-0bee577c4e6b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5388f336-ceb4-56ec-9471-62e471e77e31" "answer-69a8ea1e-5e95-555b-b649-0bee577c4e6b" "answer-26c3a655-0dec-5f7e-95cd-2db00d0dd192" "answer-d15425e8-6e9d-5ec6-b00d-cf995325a194" "answer-387b8f14-b652-57