.$ Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(5,-5),$ and its minimum value is $-5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7c7c3f4c-2644-5973-a5e9-158bd96739b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7c7c3f4c-2644-5973-a5e9-158bd96739b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "7c7c3f4c-2644-5973-a5e9-158bd96739b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ee43b3ab-3ae9-56b0-889d-5b4544f936b6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-660923f0-ae1b-56d3-b4ef-4ceb27802fda" "answer-f5f003b1-42da-5dd8-91a2-319030e9a4c2" "answer-ee43b3ab-3ae9-56b0-889d-5b4544f936b6" "answer-d2fd642a-c21c-57bd-bc62-b55691d0af0e" "answer-1008e2c3-118b-56f9-b31f-46fa861bd2d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-660923f0-ae1b-56d3-b4ef-4ceb27802fda" :answer/id #uuid "660923f0-ae1b-56d3-b4ef-4ceb27802fda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5f003b1-42da-5dd8-91a2-319030e9a4c2" :answer/id #uuid "f5f003b1-42da-5dd8-91a2-319030e9a4c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee43b3ab-3ae9-56b0-889d-5b4544f936b6" :answer/id #uuid "ee43b3ab-3ae9-56b0-889d-5b4544f936b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2fd642a-c21c-57bd-bc62-b55691d0af0e" :answer/id #uuid "d2fd642a-c21c-57bd-bc62-b55691d0af0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1008e2c3-118b-56f9-b31f-46fa861bd2d1" :answer/id #uuid "1008e2c3-118b-56f9-b31f-46fa861bd2d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b8aee55c-aa76-53a1-afb7-e8a42dc37dcd" :question/id #uuid "b8aee55c-aa76-53a1-afb7-e8a42dc37dcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-106247" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f07f2e5473df35b47f3033428fee4ae9b38cf554c00f0ddd1cb12a40d981710.png)\n\nFind the minimum value of the parabola $y=(x+1)^{2}+5$, sketched above." :question/worked-solution "The minimum value of the parabola is the $y$-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola. \n\nRemember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n$$\ny=(x+1)^{2}+5\n$$\n\nis written in vertex form with $h=-1$ and $k=5.$ Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(-1,5),$ and its minimum value is $5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-182db2a7-120c-575a-b697-10dbc867e038"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-182db2a7-120c-575a-b697-10dbc867e038" :answer-field/id #uuid "182db2a7-120c-575a-b697-10dbc867e038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b2363633-4b4a-52b6-a1e8-74d6417cb54d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2dd87c5f-688e-591f-8a52-8150a12a9667" "answer-e9dab541-f6ea-5af9-a097-2799d5494452" "answer-61eff8b4-3b45-53c5-b90f-57c84a4ca251" "answer-680c6546-17a4-56c9-bab0-a73030879071" "answer-b2363633-4b4a-52b6-a1e8-74d6417cb54d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dd87c5f-688e-591f-8a52-8150a12a9667" :answer/id #uuid "2dd87c5f-688e-591f-8a52-8150a12a9667" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9dab541-f6ea-5af9-a097-2799d5494452" :answer/id #uuid "e9dab541-f6ea-5af9-a097-2799d5494452" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-61eff8b4-3b45-53c5-b90f-57c84a4ca251" :answer/id #uuid "61eff8b4-3b45-53c5-b90f-57c84a4ca251" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-680c6546-17a4-56c9-bab0-a73030879071" :answer/id #uuid "680c6546-17a4-56c9-bab0-a73030879071" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2363633-4b4a-52b6-a1e8-74d6417cb54d" :answer/id #uuid "b2363633-4b4a-52b6-a1e8-74d6417cb54d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3968dfc0-a6cb-5c0d-a0d8-393a6ade59fc" :question/id #uuid "3968dfc0-a6cb-5c0d-a0d8-393a6ade59fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-46111" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f68edc3f727f56d9cb88b2812600e89d985a494b736e75b9ecc86ee7a9e9a278.png)\n\nFind the maximum value of the parabola $y=-(x-2)^{2}+5$, sketched above." :question/worked-solution "The maximum value of the parabola is the $y$-coordinate of the vertex of the parabola. \n\nRemember that the vertex form of a parabola is\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^{2}+k\n$$\n\nwhere $(h,k)$ are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola.\n\nThe given parabola\n\n$$\ny=-(x-2)^{2}+5\n$$\n\nis written in vertex form with $h=2$ and $k=5.$ Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(2,5),$ and its maximum value is $5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-82ef5680-ece9-5685-94ac-2157be8d10db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-82ef5680-ece9-5685-94ac-2157be8d10db" :answer-field/id #uuid "82ef5680-ece9-5685-94ac-2157be8d10db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0d4152be-7b9d-5747-b16b-5954539d8872" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4fcbbcd-789c-5058-9a7d-5def3b745cfe" "answer-954aa467-9af7-5120-bc56-8b6c2fb39aa3" "answer-91e40f28-0701-51f6-bc0e-55efb0a1c4fa" "answer-b872f23c-eb67-568f-9ee1-244b8203c4fc" "answer-0d4152be-7b9d-5747-b16b-5954539d8872"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4fcbbcd-789c-5058-9a7d-5def3b745cfe" :answer/id #uuid "e4fcbbcd-789c-5058-9a7d-5def3b745cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-954aa467-9af7-5120-bc56-8b6c2fb39aa3" :answer/id #uuid "954aa467-9af7-5120-bc56-8b6c2fb39aa3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91e40f28-0701-51f6-bc0e-55efb0a1c4fa" :answer/id #uuid "91e40f28-0701-51f6-bc0e-55efb0a1c4fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b872f23c-eb67-568f-9ee1-244b8203c4fc" :answer/id #uuid "b872f23c-eb67-568f-9ee1-244b8203c4fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d4152be-7b9d-5747-b16b-5954539d8872" :answer/id #uuid "0d4152be-7b9d-5747-b16b-5954539d8872" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088870 :tutorial/content "Up to now, all the parabolas we've seen have been written in **standard form**.\n\n![](images/e3/e3fb2f9598362bc373e475faf19bb84623a73281da549bf85018f22b7f8a095d.svg)\n\nWe can also write the equation of a parabola in the so-called **vertex form,** given by\n\n![](images/4d/4dc70525f24857744b03c32f2f558b27691c823f17bab16a569d3bbdadab732b.svg)\n\nWhen a parabola is written in vertex form, the pair ![](images/3a/3a6333dccc007be0992f95974a58ce3d652e5ecafcaaa865a24ff8b42bcfc37a.svg) gives the coordinates of its vertex.\n\nFor example, consider the following parabola: \n\n![](images/23/2366bd4f3ea2bcb500a85b13c29a3b512d0529ffc82e507c81dae3df96176981.svg)\n\nThis parabola is written in vertex form with ![](images/b4/b4edfee225234d67bf8c5a1f8283fe3eb5edba258f6268e9ffe7e604def55858.svg) and ![](images/6f/6fa7648f1eeb4effdf9b60cf5199b0ac26e1c1d57c0f05d9e9bd895e5c277c21.svg) Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at ![](images/2b/2bea2c53403b70e552b749cbbf6a3eba65ebdf56f2028ee7907a6700bbedafc4.svg)\n\n![](images/4e/4e050c9f52965d9682cae055c1264ac2db9a58b09557f6e15f0178e9375b3872.png)\n\nIn future lessons, we'll learn how to express any parabola in vertex form. For now, let's get some practice working with this form." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186088990 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our first example once more.\n\n![](images/bc/bc15538fc507a42e4ef0cde0798d90cafca6d3d02e5e7c1630c6fc3e4a1e306b.png)\n\nYou might be wondering how we can tell that the vertex coordinates are ![](images/e2/e2002597cf3d8b01e24e7b4d2c6daf6fc9afb0afb3c6140877abfc3bf1be02c4.svg) just from looking at the equation. \n\nTo see this, let's label the two terms in our parabola formula:\n\n![](images/03/03e67590d6a4c3f9257b2330fcce119e3710397453b36fb0f9486964259ba40b.svg)\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The quadratic part ![](images/36/3658549ec19c3c38654ef48e3982b82008b2630ebd0de7f5fe04bd7d4d7073ef.svg) is a *squared* quantity. Therefore, it can only be *positive* or *zero*.\n\n- Moreover, the quadratic part *equals* zero when ![](images/d3/d3f2aa545ab3eb25dc10571bd58aefec57b90fbf67e3281039c937b753b48a6a.svg)\n\n- Since the constant part ![](images/07/07e82ee914d3f32c77556663cbc38417dc5f057c180e58a1fb4230bb364f5c25.svg) does not vary with ![](images/5a/5aac2c81021f3f9e468e7840c5f95ece35c10b894bdae19ec1d247bdeb47ac6f.svg) the entire expression is *minimized* when ![](images/d3/d3f2aa545ab3eb25dc10571bd58aefec57b90fbf67e3281039c937b753b48a6a.svg) Therefore, the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-coordinate of the minimum point (i.e., the vertex) is ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)\n\n- When ![](images/48/480183a1321e5b4fdff9500a551818f6fb0ab2713c0fdd8fa9df88a640394af9.svg) the quadratic part is zero, giving ![](images/21/2182a4d19eb1c758d967edc4951247e3d512519ac6d8b95676bc51eb2fe57d1c.svg) Therefore, the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinate of the vertex is ![](images/da/daae2fd3251c4ba175906af980d1c33d4db057c616509ec6a146307d64417228.svg)\n\nFollowing this logic, we conclude that the coordinates of the vertex are ![](images/2b/2bea2c53403b70e552b749cbbf6a3eba65ebdf56f2028ee7907a6700bbedafc4.svg)\n\nFinally, it's possible to generalize this argument to any parabola. For ![](images/28/28e6012b2b10c0a7f40681ab8a73666a12afd6e976c59f536ed30d1defca5f64.svg) the coordinates ![](images/3a/3a6333dccc007be0992f95974a58ce3d652e5ecafcaaa865a24ff8b42bcfc37a.svg) give the maximum of the parabola." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186087900 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129135 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9b0cd7b7-a43d-5a47-86ed-f4c8db97a17e" "question-f594f319-f018-5c1b-a5cc-5800c5d009c3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128946 :question/problem "![](images/0f/0f341a3893509e9adc2b5897e14a87656aa3c49824dda809ae6030646c6ece79.svg)\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PCP^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2 \\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PCP^{-1},$ where \n\n![](images/48/489a26b52ac132d82ffa918d931963a0cd33df092aea8e70885dc41c4e363be5.svg)\n\nand $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda.$\n\n- From the matrix ![](images/bf/bfc2be5324b72df4c3c63e92c4501bbda6b0fcde0ef8e6284d739db51e73a722.svg) we deduce that $\\textrm{Re}(\\lambda)={\\color{blue}3}$ and $\\textrm{Im}(\\lambda) = {\\color{red}-1}.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda = {\\color{blue}3}+({\\color{red}-1})\\text{i} = 3-\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix ![](images/1f/1fd5d191e88cd5f00ffd91f83800ce0c82024cedea60bf78432cd0abb504252a.svg) we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n![](images/df/df9978ae9ef1bb16b79e36abace84ef559ef64b6aa9e47369ae7efc5ecf7da27.svg)\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $\\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n![](images/81/811faf0baa4ae081f2f5ae9064a060f979ae553df5e5e156028e91b3038a4a2b.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9b0cd7b7-a43d-5a47-86ed-f4c8db97a17e" :question/id #uuid "9b0cd7b7-a43d-5a47-86ed-f4c8db97a17e" :question/math-academy-id "q-99331" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=3.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i}=-1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b7a6cbf3-7f53-5074-aaf3-d25b540e376b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b7a6cbf3-7f53-5074-aaf3-d25b540e376b" :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a6cbf3-7f53-5074-aaf3-d25b540e376b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b29624d8-e74c-5d1a-940d-c700cbfc0544" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b29624d8-e74c-5d1a-940d-c700cbfc0544" "answer-8d7b33be-a60d-5778-8e1e-35e9819d31a6" "answer-889ef78d-7759-5373-abe0-8c890bd54b6a" "answer-2dd09250-36e7-55f0-82b8-a9e7a77f1438" "answer-ff749ce7-1514-5dc0-a1dc-80101b787592"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b29624d8-e74c-5d1a-940d-c700cbfc0544" :answer/id #uuid "b29624d8-e74c-5d1a-940d-c700cbfc0544" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d7b33be-a60d-5778-8e1e-35e9819d31a6" :answer/id #uuid "8d7b33be-a60d-5778-8e1e-35e9819d31a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-889ef78d-7759-5373-abe0-8c890bd54b6a" :answer/id #uuid "889ef78d-7759-5373-abe0-8c890bd54b6a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =1\\pm 3\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dd09250-36e7-55f0-82b8-a9e7a77f1438" :answer/id #uuid "2dd09250-36e7-55f0-82b8-a9e7a77f1438" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =3\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff749ce7-1514-5dc0-a1dc-80101b787592" :answer/id #uuid "ff749ce7-1514-5dc0-a1dc-80101b787592" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =4\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -3\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f594f319-f018-5c1b-a5cc-5800c5d009c3" :question/id #uuid "f594f319-f018-5c1b-a5cc-5800c5d009c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140493" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ what are the eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4+(-2)\\text{i}=4-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -4∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2b87a7e-c264-561d-a51e-4b9f3f87be5f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2b87a7e-c264-561d-a51e-4b9f3f87be5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2b87a7e-c264-561d-a51e-4b9f3f87be5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-020f1059-0827-5cff-b5d3-3b27a0b3f7cf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a99c7cef-8132-5f5f-8a42-7e03f3072eef" "answer-940c4d08-310e-5fa6-8255-e8b00c097567" "answer-020f1059-0827-5cff-b5d3-3b27a0b3f7cf" "answer-b67d16fd-23ae-5931-8be6-5092894af74e" "answer-45961fa9-10ea-50be-affe-76931d1697fc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a99c7cef-8132-5f5f-8a42-7e03f3072eef" :answer/id #uuid "a99c7cef-8132-5f5f-8a42-7e03f3072eef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =2\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1∓4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-940c4d08-310e-5fa6-8255-e8b00c097567" :answer/id #uuid "940c4d08-310e-5fa6-8255-e8b00c097567" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =2\\pm \\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-020f1059-0827-5cff-b5d3-3b27a0b3f7cf" :answer/id #uuid "020f1059-0827-5cff-b5d3-3b27a0b3f7cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -4∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b67d16fd-23ae-5931-8be6-5092894af74e" :answer/id #uuid "b67d16fd-23ae-5931-8be6-5092894af74e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45961fa9-10ea-50be-affe-76931d1697fc" :answer/id #uuid "45961fa9-10ea-50be-affe-76931d1697fc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\pm \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129134 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fed17ade-b98f-5b7e-aa56-3747d2eae8ab"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fed17ade-b98f-5b7e-aa56-3747d2eae8ab" :question/id #uuid "fed17ade-b98f-5b7e-aa56-3747d2eae8ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-99343" :question/problem "$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $v$ and the rotation-scaling matrix $C$ shown above. Given that $v$ is an eigenvector of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3-2\\text{i},$ find a matrix $P$ such that $A=PC{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nFirst, notice that \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]=C.\n$$\n\nNext, we can find the real and imaginary parts of our eigenvector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =[\\begin{aligned}-4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]={{[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Re}(v)}+\\text{i}{{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Im}(v)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now construct $P:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0fea878e-a991-5b88-82fa-0dff5d838738"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0fea878e-a991-5b88-82fa-0dff5d838738" :answer-field/id #uuid "0fea878e-a991-5b88-82fa-0dff5d838738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-12fab1be-92c3-5c73-9057-2ce01cfa87cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bd5d726b-4c91-5de1-b99c-cbf38e533e45" "answer-66b397cb-7b81-5983-a6b0-49e027e16f2b" "answer-12fab1be-92c3-5c73-9057-2ce01cfa87cd" "answer-1b236a4d-fc2f-53b6-af86-4c4f9793792b" "answer-4ca133f2-63fd-5e30-bf07-bb0ae8d8988a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd5d726b-4c91-5de1-b99c-cbf38e533e45" :answer/id #uuid "bd5d726b-4c91-5de1-b99c-cbf38e533e45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66b397cb-7b81-5983-a6b0-49e027e16f2b" :answer/id #uuid "66b397cb-7b81-5983-a6b0-49e027e16f2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12fab1be-92c3-5c73-9057-2ce01cfa87cd" :answer/id #uuid "12fab1be-92c3-5c73-9057-2ce01cfa87cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b236a4d-fc2f-53b6-af86-4c4f9793792b" :answer/id #uuid "1b236a4d-fc2f-53b6-af86-4c4f9793792b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ca133f2-63fd-5e30-bf07-bb0ae8d8988a" :answer/id #uuid "4ca133f2-63fd-5e30-bf07-bb0ae8d8988a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129136 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7eede828-b363-5824-aef0-9e1007418c88"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7eede828-b363-5824-aef0-9e1007418c88" :question/id #uuid "7eede828-b363-5824-aef0-9e1007418c88" :question/math-academy-id "q-99476" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above.\nGiven that $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where $C$ is a rotation-scaling matrix, what is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2+\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=2+\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -2 \\\\ 13 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5-\\text{i} & -2 \\\\ 13 & -5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 13{x}_{1}+(-5-\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(5-\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}{{5}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d6b92f20-ac1b-5794-9dbb-0cc54d1d0397"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6b92f20-ac1b-5794-9dbb-0cc54d1d0397" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6b92f20-ac1b-5794-9dbb-0cc54d1d0397" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9fec3ede-dd6e-545e-8574-c51f46483d73" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b90271d8-7502-571d-8474-c5e46ce637f4" "answer-a4b9c6fe-27e8-5acb-af1b-5a95bd5cdb89" "answer-9fec3ede-dd6e-545e-8574-c51f46483d73" "answer-31b1ab74-70b5-55c5-8ff9-594e80672f5b" "answer-15624df2-9887-5ec3-8e91-fe7710aba543"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b90271d8-7502-571d-8474-c5e46ce637f4" :answer/id #uuid "b90271d8-7502-571d-8474-c5e46ce637f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4b9c6fe-27e8-5acb-af1b-5a95bd5cdb89" :answer/id #uuid "a4b9c6fe-27e8-5acb-af1b-5a95bd5cdb89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{26}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fec3ede-dd6e-545e-8574-c51f46483d73" :answer/id #uuid "9fec3ede-dd6e-545e-8574-c51f46483d73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31b1ab74-70b5-55c5-8ff9-594e80672f5b" :answer/id #uuid "31b1ab74-70b5-55c5-8ff9-594e80672f5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15624df2-9887-5ec3-8e91-fe7710aba543" :answer/id #uuid "15624df2-9887-5ec3-8e91-fe7710aba543" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{26}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130353 :tutorial/content "Rotation-scaling matrices are involved in an important theorem regarding complex eigenvalues.\n\nTo sketch out the key idea, let's consider the matrix \n\n![](images/06/06a28b9b9d8db65a4214ed1d446c68f288978acc59afd3720873da7f825efe13.svg)\n\nwhich has the complex eigenvalue \n\n$$\n\\lambda=1-2\\textrm{i}={\\color{blue}1}+({\\color{red}-2})\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding complex eigenvector \n\n![](images/1b/1b7aa45404b3e6e02cb9802da371f6c74f6848f4b5601261f2257e12b7d362d0.svg)\n\nNow, we construct a rotation-scaling matrix $C$ using the real and imaginary parts of the eigenvalue $\\lambda$ as follows:\n![](images/0f/0f29362b05dd18b6a8a307f531cea6d59c083301703285bde41a9488f228c386.svg)\nWe also construct a matrix $P$ whose columns are the real and imaginary parts of the corresponding eigenvector:\n![](images/d1/d1b2efc5b22e34c0bb4cb93a4777c8d8139ca0047253b7177bd1809a272f5b37.svg)\n\nThe **rotation-scaling theorem** states that $A$ can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\nA=PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet's check that this is true in this case:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}PC{P}^{-1} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -10 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =A\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130354 :tutorial/content "Let's now formally state the rotation-scaling theorem:\n\n*Let $A$ be a $2 \\times 2$ matrix with real entries. If $\\lambda$ is a complex (not real) eigenvalue of $A,$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda,$ then *\n\n$$\nA = PCP^{-1}\n$$\n\n*where*\n![](images/74/74b63ce33cfa7779a6668a22974b709d549843a221c3b13067887e8193c6e6f0.svg)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- When two matrices $A$ and $C$ are related via the equation $A = PCP^{-1},$ we say that they are **similar.**\n\n- The rotation-scaling theorem tells us that every $2 \\!\\times\\! 2$ real matrix with complex (non-real) eigenvalues is similar to a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We call the right-hand side of $A = PCP^{-1}$ the **rotation-scaling form** of $A.$ We can also refer to this as the **rotation-scaling decomposition** of $A.$\n\n- The matrix $P$ *must* be constructed from the eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue defined by $C.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130355 :tutorial/content "Suppose $A$ is a real $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrix. To interpret the rotation-scaling form $A=PCP^{-1},$ we should bear in mind the following:\n\n- The matrix $C$ rotates vectors $\\mathbf x\\in\\mathbb R^2$ and then scales them.\n\n- We can think of $P$ as representing the following change of variable:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x = P\\mathbf u \\qquad \\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad  \\mathbf u = P^{-1}\\mathbf x\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ can be thought of as a change of variable from $\\mathbf x$ to $\\mathbf u,$ followed by a rotation and scaling before returning to the original variable.\n\n![](images/ab/ab40869137296cea63fe209a842a4713fcae4fbf53c5089e6722b70bdf3f3230.png)\n\nFor example, let's consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/74/74c17c58d6b39ff6a13ec56e135ec17ce0f260d2e9d90e562544637f6e677db0.svg)\n\nThis is a real matrix with complex eigenvalues and is thus similar to a rotation-scaling matrix. \n\nUsing the techniques described in this lesson, we can show that the rotation-scaling decomposition of $A$ is\n\n![](images/15/1542ac2d3f79e1494ecf9e67dda3219966efdf76267ebda82d7689555dc4df30.svg)\nThe matrix $C$ can be written explicitly as a constant multiple of a rotation matrix as follows:\n![](images/f0/f0d562472e2b230a0cd393829ab4a5dec380e6fd1e2a797af4f1f651aae88f75.svg)\n\nThus, the matrix $C$ represents the following:\n\n- a rotation of $\\arccos\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt 3}{2}\\right) = \\dfrac\\pi 6$ counterclockwise about $O,$ and\n\n- an enlargement of scale factor ${\\color{blue}{2}}.$\n\nThe image of a unit square centered at the origin under the action of $A$ can therefore be decomposed as follows:\n\n![](images/92/92f2ab84b9ac8f36a93c42bf93b70b08a3e64eb3920a8bd2d5276217d936ab79.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129361 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157269 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4de28170-b86e-539e-8022-1a0054f8d5b3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4de28170-b86e-539e-8022-1a0054f8d5b3" :question/id #uuid "4de28170-b86e-539e-8022-1a0054f8d5b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-253260" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3{J}_{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,$ $n=2,$ and $\\lambda =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nSince $n=2,$ we need to compute the first $2-1=1$ derivative of $f:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 0 & f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-34f645a6-64ef-5792-b6c7-1a4f744bf319" "answer-field-ab8fbdbc-05a9-5707-ab78-71fcfbb42736" "answer-field-d0bee359-1a75-5e2f-8f5b-e36a4511f781" "answer-field-32456fdc-62d9-5dbb-a411-ba3d281fd124"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-34f645a6-64ef-5792-b6c7-1a4f744bf319" :answer-field/id #uuid "34f645a6-64ef-5792-b6c7-1a4f744bf319" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f672b119-050a-5608-9c83-4c9132443e95" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f672b119-050a-5608-9c83-4c9132443e95"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f672b119-050a-5608-9c83-4c9132443e95" :answer/id #uuid "f672b119-050a-5608-9c83-4c9132443e95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab8fbdbc-05a9-5707-ab78-71fcfbb42736" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8fbdbc-05a9-5707-ab78-71fcfbb42736" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e333e92e-a591-5e20-9ca0-01be53fe0055" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e333e92e-a591-5e20-9ca0-01be53fe0055"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e333e92e-a591-5e20-9ca0-01be53fe0055" :answer/id #uuid "e333e92e-a591-5e20-9ca0-01be53fe0055" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d0bee359-1a75-5e2f-8f5b-e36a4511f781" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0bee359-1a75-5e2f-8f5b-e36a4511f781" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2725edec-e9e4-5fb5-993b-32b7e209acd0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2725edec-e9e4-5fb5-993b-32b7e209acd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2725edec-e9e4-5fb5-993b-32b7e209acd0" :answer/id #uuid "2725edec-e9e4-5fb5-993b-32b7e209acd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-32456fdc-62d9-5dbb-a411-ba3d281fd124" :answer-field/id #uuid "32456fdc-62d9-5dbb-a411-ba3d281fd124" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-0e7b951f-d98e-5a68-8472-6e057c0ab5a6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0e7b951f-d98e-5a68-8472-6e057c0ab5a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e7b951f-d98e-5a68-8472-6e057c0ab5a6" :answer/id #uuid "0e7b951f-d98e-5a68-8472-6e057c0ab5a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157271 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a906d22b-042f-5736-a4c7-747a5da98697"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-a906d22b-042f-5736-a4c7-747a5da98697" :question/id #uuid "a906d22b-042f-5736-a4c7-747a5da98697" :question/math-academy-id "q-253490" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{6} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{5\\pi }{6} & \\pi \\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\pi \\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $E=PD{P}^{-1},$ find $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡E.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and matrices $A$ and $S,$ where $S$ is invertible, we have\n\n$$\nf(SA{S}^{-1})=S\\cdot f(A)\\cdot {S}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡E$ and the given matrix $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡E=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(PD{P}^{-1})=P\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $D$ is a diagonal matrix, we get\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡D=[\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi \\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAlso, the inverse of $P$ is\n\n$$\n{P}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡E & =P\\cdot (4\\operatorname{sin}⁡D)\\cdot {P}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-85a7cfaa-2021-5878-b715-b12be6d3a7f4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-85a7cfaa-2021-5878-b715-b12be6d3a7f4" :answer-field/id #uuid "85a7cfaa-2021-5878-b715-b12be6d3a7f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-650f951f-a1c7-5370-9079-613e1ed08f22" :answer-field/choices ["answer-613b1292-599e-5175-a4de-dac872a76495" "answer-69eb12dd-2688-5a53-91ae-fe2035e9de63" "answer-d2c5f2de-8e97-5e34-b223-ff08b5bc179e" "answer-650f951f-a1c7-5370-9079-613e1ed08f22" "answer-70bc40d1-370d-5f28-96fb-58c2c278fc37"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-613b1292-599e-5175-a4de-dac872a76495" :answer/id #uuid "613b1292-599e-5175-a4de-dac872a76495" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-69eb12dd-2688-5a53-91ae-fe2035e9de63" :answer/id #uuid "69eb12dd-2688-5a53-91ae-fe2035e9de63" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d2c5f2de-8e97-5e34-b223-ff08b5bc179e" :answer/id #uuid "d2c5f2de-8e97-5e34-b223-ff08b5bc179e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-650f951f-a1c7-5370-9079-613e1ed08f22" :answer/id #uuid "650f951f-a1c7-5370-9079-613e1ed08f22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70bc40d1-370d-5f28-96fb-58c2c278fc37" :answer/id #uuid "70bc40d1-370d-5f28-96fb-58c2c278fc37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157270 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-59f14881-731e-52d2-9146-dab6e581e8a4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-59f14881-731e-52d2-9146-dab6e581e8a4" :question/id #uuid "59f14881-731e-52d2-9146-dab6e581e8a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-253868" :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" :question/worked-solution "Given a function $f$ and a Jordan block ${J}_{n}(\\lambda ),$ we have\n\n$$\nf({J}_{n}(\\lambda ))=[\\begin{aligned}f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & \\frac{{f}^{″}(\\lambda )}{2!} & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-1)}(\\lambda )}{(n-1)!} \\\\ 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) & ⋯ & \\frac{{f}^{(n-2)}(\\lambda )}{(n-2)!} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋱ & ⋮ \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda ) & {f}^{′}(\\lambda ) \\\\ 0 & ⋯ & ⋯ & 0 & f(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡([\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(2) & 0 \\\\ 0 & {J}_{1}(2)\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)) & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2))\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the function of each Jordan block separately.\n\n- For ${J}_{2}(2),$ we have $n=2$ and $\\lambda =2.$ \\since $n=2,$ we need to compute the first derivative of $f$ only:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{2}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(2) & {f}^{′}(2) \\\\ 0 & f(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For ${J}_{1}(2),$ we have $n=1$ and $\\lambda =1.$ \\since $n=1,$ we don't need any derivatives. So, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({J}_{1}(2)) & =[\\begin{aligned}f(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{2}(2))\\,⊕\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡({J}_{1}(2)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & \\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d18814a4-c3b7-50c0-86f8-c7a6e058383a" "answer-field-250a0410-56cf-50a6-9de1-7caaa7d5a608" "answer-field-8a725e18-61fa-5fa6-82d0-ff96754212ab" "answer-field-c78abd9f-d0b3-531a-96cd-b8ea8b5d6311" "answer-field-bc31d784-bb6f-5691-9bf1-b7d09bc77e17"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d18814a4-c3b7-50c0-86f8-c7a6e058383a" :answer-field/id #uuid "d18814a4-c3b7-50c0-86f8-c7a6e058383a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-83d08973-12e3-5a13-bcc4-7e7a8399498d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83d08973-12e3-5a13-bcc4-7e7a8399498d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83d08973-12e3-5a13-bcc4-7e7a8399498d" :answer/id #uuid "83d08973-12e3-5a13-bcc4-7e7a8399498d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\ln(2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-250a0410-56cf-50a6-9de1-7caaa7d5a608" :answer-field/id #uuid "250a0410-56cf-50a6-9de1-7caaa7d5a608" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ea0401e5-9d3a-57a5-b046-48a14ed96f7c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ea0401e5-9d3a-57a5-b046-48a14ed96f7c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea0401e5-9d3a-57a5-b046-48a14ed96f7c" :answer/id #uuid "ea0401e5-9d3a-57a5-b046-48a14ed96f7c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8a725e18-61fa-5fa6-82d0-ff96754212ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "8a725e18-61fa-5fa6-82d0-ff96754212ab" :answer-field/key 