8x+11.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=2>0,$ $b=8,$ and $c=11.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{8}{2\\cdot 2}=-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=-2$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}+8x+11 \\\\  & =2(-2)^{2}+8(-2)+11 \\\\  & =8-16+11 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-2,3),$ and the range of the function is $[3,\\infty ),$ i.e. $3\\le f(x)<\\infty .$\n\n![](images/3a/3a87830f59bf840ac8ab0d761ab7319b53fc804bc9d925e92850313d5d5a7a52.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ea59e43-3e9e-551b-b8f4-d4b59960c0d1" "answer-field-3cb67adb-362a-502d-ba97-5bf0f0b88ad1" "answer-field-09ce1543-bab3-5287-8403-e2b7bf000729"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ea59e43-3e9e-551b-b8f4-d4b59960c0d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea59e43-3e9e-551b-b8f4-d4b59960c0d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3b8db516-3ec3-52ed-bcd3-204a1eac8e73" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3b8db516-3ec3-52ed-bcd3-204a1eac8e73"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b8db516-3ec3-52ed-bcd3-204a1eac8e73" :answer/id #uuid "3b8db516-3ec3-52ed-bcd3-204a1eac8e73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3cb67adb-362a-502d-ba97-5bf0f0b88ad1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb67adb-362a-502d-ba97-5bf0f0b88ad1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-17a85056-0a28-50b2-a9dc-d58df89de9a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-17a85056-0a28-50b2-a9dc-d58df89de9a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17a85056-0a28-50b2-a9dc-d58df89de9a3" :answer/id #uuid "17a85056-0a28-50b2-a9dc-d58df89de9a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-09ce1543-bab3-5287-8403-e2b7bf000729" :answer-field/id #uuid "09ce1543-bab3-5287-8403-e2b7bf000729" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-c9125bed-e59b-5820-bb19-1b272efd5b76" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c9125bed-e59b-5820-bb19-1b272efd5b76"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c9125bed-e59b-5820-bb19-1b272efd5b76" :answer/id #uuid "c9125bed-e59b-5820-bb19-1b272efd5b76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-16afc9a4-390c-580b-b4f5-9f8ca64be1a7" :question/id #uuid "16afc9a4-390c-580b-b4f5-9f8ca64be1a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-154270" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-6x+5?$" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=3>0,$ $b=-6,$ and $c=5.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-6)}{2\\cdot 3}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3{x}^{2}-6x+5 \\\\  & =3(1)^{2}-6(1)+5 \\\\  & =3-6+5 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(1,2),$ and the range of the function is $[2,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/91/915c1435f78f8a5f85a104b40d9ee83c54c218f8e81b6ea7fc051367055fc624.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc73fd49-ad93-51ca-81e7-7caa58db6c5f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc73fd49-ad93-51ca-81e7-7caa58db6c5f" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc73fd49-ad93-51ca-81e7-7caa58db6c5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a1fd0b4a-458f-56d9-a72b-4fcec594a2bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d93ed979-a31a-5d1a-a93c-591126feb602" "answer-6e2f1db6-cccc-5d24-9a2d-142ff1ee9a9c" "answer-9d4e9999-7fc5-563b-b703-5c6492439ade" "answer-0541d29e-1a3d-5725-a3e8-30e78b5ddec7" "answer-a1fd0b4a-458f-56d9-a72b-4fcec594a2bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d93ed979-a31a-5d1a-a93c-591126feb602" :answer/id #uuid "d93ed979-a31a-5d1a-a93c-591126feb602" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-2]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e2f1db6-cccc-5d24-9a2d-142ff1ee9a9c" :answer/id #uuid "6e2f1db6-cccc-5d24-9a2d-142ff1ee9a9c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,1]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d4e9999-7fc5-563b-b703-5c6492439ade" :answer/id #uuid "9d4e9999-7fc5-563b-b703-5c6492439ade" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-1,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0541d29e-1a3d-5725-a3e8-30e78b5ddec7" :answer/id #uuid "0541d29e-1a3d-5725-a3e8-30e78b5ddec7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1fd0b4a-458f-56d9-a72b-4fcec594a2bf" :answer/id #uuid "a1fd0b4a-458f-56d9-a72b-4fcec594a2bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-89ea5a4a-e490-551f-988c-728a0c124eab" :question/id #uuid "89ea5a4a-e490-551f-988c-728a0c124eab" :question/math-academy-id "q-250639" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+4x+1.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$<f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=-1<0,$ $b=4,$ and $c=1.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{4}{2\\cdot (-1)}=2.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=2$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{x}^{2}+4x+1 \\\\  & =-(2)^{2}+4(2)+1 \\\\  & =-4+8+1 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(2,5),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,5],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 5.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce52355662b526af83614a6c4cbe6e7f2d7f07d379b2636d14e094d47e0e0da6.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8e23828e-dbe8-55aa-ba5d-1021ce14ba4b" "answer-field-788caaeb-874f-5a58-a638-27770e22a12b" "answer-field-c1c47709-4fb4-5410-8bb2-077d0d2f6c96"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8e23828e-dbe8-55aa-ba5d-1021ce14ba4b" :answer-field/id #uuid "8e23828e-dbe8-55aa-ba5d-1021ce14ba4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-560383cf-7bf3-5f2c-8cdd-a33f83f8439c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-560383cf-7bf3-5f2c-8cdd-a33f83f8439c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-560383cf-7bf3-5f2c-8cdd-a33f83f8439c" :answer/id #uuid "560383cf-7bf3-5f2c-8cdd-a33f83f8439c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-788caaeb-874f-5a58-a638-27770e22a12b" :answer-field/id #uuid "788caaeb-874f-5a58-a638-27770e22a12b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6431dec3-2744-5c91-9888-35fb8d486a49" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6431dec3-2744-5c91-9888-35fb8d486a49"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6431dec3-2744-5c91-9888-35fb8d486a49" :answer/id #uuid "6431dec3-2744-5c91-9888-35fb8d486a49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\le" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c1c47709-4fb4-5410-8bb2-077d0d2f6c96" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c47709-4fb4-5410-8bb2-077d0d2f6c96" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-35735545-6764-5af4-8b30-c44340961668" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35735545-6764-5af4-8b30-c44340961668"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35735545-6764-5af4-8b30-c44340961668" :answer/id #uuid "35735545-6764-5af4-8b30-c44340961668" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125237 :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-4x+5?$" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in standard form,\n\n$$\ny=a{x}^{2}+bx+c,\n$$\n\nwith $a=2>0,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=5.$\n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is given by\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{b}{2a}=-\\frac{(-4)}{2\\cdot 2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=1$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2{x}^{2}-4x+5 \\\\  & =2(1)^{2}-4(1)+5 \\\\  & =2-4+5 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(1,3),$ and the range of the function is $[3,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/a8/a8fc15df2b24054485bb07330f40919ebf13cb9969073a733b9426a28dab20b5.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124022 :answer-field/correct "answer-e6a97956-8fdf-547f-bcc6-039d7a0099a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-81c5bbf2-cb3f-5027-93c7-adfd59d8054d" "answer-ba14fcfa-ffa6-571d-88a3-f4da05a8b41d" "answer-abd657a4-c200-5733-9a0a-52aadbe8c41c" "answer-e6a97956-8fdf-547f-bcc6-039d7a0099a2" "answer-f39445c2-f0a2-5251-85ea-f5894b6372ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81c5bbf2-cb3f-5027-93c7-adfd59d8054d" :answer/id #uuid "81c5bbf2-cb3f-5027-93c7-adfd59d8054d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,3]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ba14fcfa-ffa6-571d-88a3-f4da05a8b41d" :answer/id #uuid "ba14fcfa-ffa6-571d-88a3-f4da05a8b41d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,5]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-abd657a4-c200-5733-9a0a-52aadbe8c41c" :answer/id #uuid "abd657a4-c200-5733-9a0a-52aadbe8c41c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6a97956-8fdf-547f-bcc6-039d7a0099a2" :answer/id #uuid "e6a97956-8fdf-547f-bcc6-039d7a0099a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[3,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f39445c2-f0a2-5251-85ea-f5894b6372ed" :answer/id #uuid "f39445c2-f0a2-5251-85ea-f5894b6372ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[5,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-da80a3c6-7cc5-5c7d-84d3-2b9e185bcdef" :question/id #uuid "da80a3c6-7cc5-5c7d-84d3-2b9e185bcdef" :question/math-academy-id "q-90989" :question/problem "What is the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)(x+6)?$" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{1}{2}>0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=-6,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{2+(-6)}{2}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=-2$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)(x+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-2-2)(-2+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-4)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-2,-8),$ and the range of the function is $[-8,\\infty ).$\n\n![](images/ba/bac4e1a79c32d61d6bc348564b2d791a06d19b0126c49af0689dd821d5063b4e.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bd54b7e3-2076-5436-90fa-bb06bb950450"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bd54b7e3-2076-5436-90fa-bb06bb950450" :answer-field/id #uuid "bd54b7e3-2076-5436-90fa-bb06bb950450" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fcb79e4a-a3fc-58ba-8a56-dedf17d975a8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-70c6a83b-dcfd-5c3d-bc2b-e9915f5cdd8f" "answer-ac845ab8-38a7-5044-9f5b-4864e79bb7a7" "answer-e0faab00-b0a3-584d-8c56-e28428306bab" "answer-fcb79e4a-a3fc-58ba-8a56-dedf17d975a8" "answer-b852e5bc-7670-5fa0-842e-aa867be2b474"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70c6a83b-dcfd-5c3d-bc2b-e9915f5cdd8f" :answer/id #uuid "70c6a83b-dcfd-5c3d-bc2b-e9915f5cdd8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,-8]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ac845ab8-38a7-5044-9f5b-4864e79bb7a7" :answer/id #uuid "ac845ab8-38a7-5044-9f5b-4864e79bb7a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-\\infty ,6]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e0faab00-b0a3-584d-8c56-e28428306bab" :answer/id #uuid "e0faab00-b0a3-584d-8c56-e28428306bab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-2,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fcb79e4a-a3fc-58ba-8a56-dedf17d975a8" :answer/id #uuid "fcb79e4a-a3fc-58ba-8a56-dedf17d975a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[-8,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b852e5bc-7670-5fa0-842e-aa867be2b474" :answer/id #uuid "b852e5bc-7670-5fa0-842e-aa867be2b474" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[6,\\infty )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8a31f28b-9e61-5b12-8422-c1bd2c17fb23" :question/id #uuid "8a31f28b-9e61-5b12-8422-c1bd2c17fb23" :question/math-academy-id "q-250644" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=(x-2)(x-8).$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)<$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=1>0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=2$ and ${x}_{2}=8,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{2+8}{2}=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=5$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(x-2)(x-8) \\\\  & =(5-2)(5-8) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(5,-9),$ and the range of the function is $[-9,\\infty ),$ i.e. $-9\\le f(x)<\\infty .$\n\n![](images/84/84c22aee72ebd52e3dab84a72ad1d402813c3a38794c653cd4cb0e31bfef3836.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-453c5296-23d3-57ab-bead-b0c23a1b99d7" "answer-field-cf3e671d-1011-5362-908e-3f28855f4af3" "answer-field-7323c510-56c5-5c7a-8032-b64a15edc529"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-453c5296-23d3-57ab-bead-b0c23a1b99d7" :answer-field/id #uuid "453c5296-23d3-57ab-bead-b0c23a1b99d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-78afb764-e323-511a-a2f2-0a8264faacfd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-78afb764-e323-511a-a2f2-0a8264faacfd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78afb764-e323-511a-a2f2-0a8264faacfd" :answer/id #uuid "78afb764-e323-511a-a2f2-0a8264faacfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cf3e671d-1011-5362-908e-3f28855f4af3" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3e671d-1011-5362-908e-3f28855f4af3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6a2fff73-ed51-5034-b645-c0efebc9c710" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6a2fff73-ed51-5034-b645-c0efebc9c710"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a2fff73-ed51-5034-b645-c0efebc9c710" :answer/id #uuid "6a2fff73-ed51-5034-b645-c0efebc9c710" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\le" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7323c510-56c5-5c7a-8032-b64a15edc529" :answer-field/id #uuid "7323c510-56c5-5c7a-8032-b64a15edc529" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-6c978228-4f12-5690-9c85-cedfcd98d83e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6c978228-4f12-5690-9c85-cedfcd98d83e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6c978228-4f12-5690-9c85-cedfcd98d83e" :answer/id #uuid "6c978228-4f12-5690-9c85-cedfcd98d83e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fe465ece-00d7-560c-ae8f-64c0313299b0" :question/id #uuid "fe465ece-00d7-560c-ae8f-64c0313299b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-250645" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{5}(x+9)(x-1).$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le f(x)$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{1}{5}>0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is positive, the parabola opens upward. Consequently, there is no maximum output, and the vertex corresponds to the minimum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-9$ and ${x}_{2}=1,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{-9+1}{2}=-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=-4$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{5}(x+9)(x-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(-4+9)(-4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 5\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(-4,-5),$ and the range of the function is $[-5,\\infty ),$ i.e. $-5\\le f(x)<\\infty .$\n\n![](images/97/97c587abebf09ff27a805db3b32b3c5f26b5d51baaf7f6827618e01ccdd53642.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-61e49312-09ef-5c75-8f30-c37d021b2a52" "answer-field-00a2dd0c-399e-5d39-b0f4-c489761a9a1d" "answer-field-7ca70ad5-e447-592a-96e6-edc3042e22c9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-61e49312-09ef-5c75-8f30-c37d021b2a52" :answer-field/id #uuid "61e49312-09ef-5c75-8f30-c37d021b2a52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7870eb48-797a-5ba2-9a14-64ab4a64026e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7870eb48-797a-5ba2-9a14-64ab4a64026e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7870eb48-797a-5ba2-9a14-64ab4a64026e" :answer/id #uuid "7870eb48-797a-5ba2-9a14-64ab4a64026e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-00a2dd0c-399e-5d39-b0f4-c489761a9a1d" :answer-field/id #uuid "00a2dd0c-399e-5d39-b0f4-c489761a9a1d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a4df160b-dd1f-5ade-944d-c37298bccec1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a4df160b-dd1f-5ade-944d-c37298bccec1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4df160b-dd1f-5ade-944d-c37298bccec1" :answer/id #uuid "a4df160b-dd1f-5ade-944d-c37298bccec1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7ca70ad5-e447-592a-96e6-edc3042e22c9" :answer-field/id #uuid "7ca70ad5-e447-592a-96e6-edc3042e22c9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b0fc840b-5156-5f1d-b747-a181692da338" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b0fc840b-5156-5f1d-b747-a181692da338"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0fc840b-5156-5f1d-b747-a181692da338" :answer/id #uuid "b0fc840b-5156-5f1d-b747-a181692da338" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8f69a422-81e0-551f-abc6-71d3ea93c826" :question/id #uuid "8f69a422-81e0-551f-abc6-71d3ea93c826" :question/math-academy-id "q-250649" :question/problem "Find the range of the function $f(x)=-4(x-1)(x-4).$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$f(x)\\le $\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "The given parabola is written in factored form,\n\n$$\ny=a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2}),\n$$\n\nwith $a=-4<0.$ \n\nBecause the leading coefficient is negative, the parabola opens downward. Consequently, there is no minimum output, and the vertex corresponds to the maximum output.\n\nThe two roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=1$ and ${x}_{2}=4,$ and we can find the $x$-coordinate of the vertex by taking the average of the roots:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{1+4}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the function to find the $y$-value of the vertex:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4(x-1)(x-4) \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{5}{2}-1)(\\frac{5}{2}-4) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertex is $(\\frac{5}{2},9),$ and the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,9],$ i.e. $-\\infty <f(x)\\le 9.$\n\n![](images/70/70dc863f5f089c9434d783495c31b77fd7a0ac84a852d2b32222b445f463efe3.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e7e6e4e4-ba72-5524-86c2-1327a0ed5696" "answer-field-35f138a0-b129-5c92-b044-9a7f9862134f" "answer-field-3cb408db-786a-5b26-a5af-0f25b4b11d35"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e7e6e4e4-ba72-5524-86c2-1327a0ed5696" :answer-field/id #uuid "e7e6e4e4-ba72-5524-86c2-1327a0ed5696" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-cf0f59aa-7331-50e4-b1d5-d86e0389fe30" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cf0f59aa-7331-50e4-b1d5-d86e0389fe30"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf0f59aa-7331-50e4-b1d5-d86e0389fe30" :answer/id #uuid "cf0f59aa-7331-50e4-b1d5-d86e0389fe30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35f138a0-b129-5c92-b044-9a7f9862134f" :answer-field/id #uuid "35f138a0-b129-5c92-b044-9a7f9862134f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3c44ff17-61f3-54f4-b363-ea1bd3f109e0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c44ff17-61f3-54f4-b363-ea1bd3f109e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c44ff17-61f3-54f4-b363-ea1bd3f109e0" :answer/id #uuid "3c44ff17-61f3-54f4-b363-ea1bd3f109e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "<" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3cb408db-786a-5b26-a5af-0f25b4b11d35" :answer-field/id #uuid "3cb408db-786a-5b26-a5af-0f25b4b11d35" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-27679f0a-767b-58cd-8144-921030108067" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27679f0a-767b-58cd-8144-921030108067"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27679f0a-767b-58cd-8144-921030108067" :answer/id #uuid "27679f0a-767b-58cd-8144-921030108067" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dc7d6aa1-177c-5e42-a678-f62831d377fa" "question-7987b888-d840-5cef-9095-39b390eecef3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9b520f9a-26c5-5349-931d-9a87c3770c07" "question-e88bf6aa-56d0-5447-b3d1-8bec7e8eb9d1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b2f7fefa-e39c-5728-9247-538da8dbc2c9" "question-be0311ba-56bf-5adc-9620-b550fa934c05"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dc7d6aa1-177c-5e42-a678-f62831d377fa" :question/id #uuid "dc7d6aa1-177c-5e42-a678-f62831d377fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-336864" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{8})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{8}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{8}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $5$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(5{x}^{8})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{8})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e5c298f-1969-5828-93b7-b6ec5738de4f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e5c298f-1969-5828-93b7-b6ec5738de4f" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e5c298f-1969-5828-93b7-b6ec5738de4f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bce09fa5-f711-56c7-921c-dd09238f7098" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bce09fa5-f711-56c7-921c-dd09238f7098"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bce09fa5-f711-56c7-921c-dd09238f7098" :answer/id #uuid "bce09fa5-f711-56c7-921c-dd09238f7098" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7987b888-d840-5cef-9095-39b390eecef3" :question/id #uuid "7987b888-d840-5cef-9095-39b390eecef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-300758" :question/problem "If $p(x)=-2{x}^{4}$ and $q(x)=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(-2{x}^{4})}{(-\\frac{8}{{x}^{6}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{10}}{4} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{10}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{4}$ does not affect the limit at infinity So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{10}={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{10})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ec63b2c8-7a90-5b2e-aad9-f70f82165d1c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ec63b2c8-7a90-5b2e-aad9-f70f82165d1c" :answer-field/id #uuid "ec63b2c8-7a90-5b2e-aad9-f70f82165d1c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7740ff6c-f994-55ad-a058-c084db22a725" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7740ff6c-f994-55ad-a058-c084db22a725"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7740ff6c-f994-55ad-a058-c084db22a725" :answer/id #uuid "7740ff6c-f994-55ad-a058-c084db22a725" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9b520f9a-26c5-5349-931d-9a87c3770c07" :question/id #uuid "9b520f9a-26c5-5349-931d-9a87c3770c07" :question/math-academy-id "q-35612" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=q(x)=-3{x}^{3},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{3}(x)\\cdot {q}^{2}(x))?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the powers and multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({p}^{3}(x)\\cdot {q}^{2}(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(p(x))}^{3}\\cdot {(q(x))}^{2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({(-3{x}^{3})}^{3}\\cdot {(-3{x}^{3})}^{2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-27{x}^{9}\\cdot 9{x}^{6}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }((-27\\cdot 9){x}^{9+6}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-243\\cdot {x}^{15}) \\\\  & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{15}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-243$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & =-243\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{15} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-243{x}^{15})=-\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-304ab48c-09a2-55aa-ab3e-0e07f921b28b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-304ab48c-09a2-55aa-ab3e-0e07f921b28b" :answer-field/id #uuid "304ab48c-09a2-55aa-ab3e-0e07f921b28b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-25578ecd-9194-5319-a63e-979c98387470" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8fc8aa42-2cbe-56b2-8cd8-103a778bb6c7" "answer-25578ecd-9194-5319-a63e-979c98387470" "answer-0495d9ff-1222-5097-80a1-d2c8e3522b4a" "answer-71026400-c6fb-5c50-8a9e-8626cef1e343" "answer-2d43a0d1-25dc-5b09-b9c3-641cd88bdb66"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fc8aa42-2cbe-56b2-8cd8-103a778bb6c7" :answer/id #uuid "8fc8aa42-2cbe-56b2-8cd8-103a778bb6c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-243" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25578ecd-9194-5319-a63e-979c98387470" :answer/id #uuid "25578ecd-9194-5319-a63e-979c98387470" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0495d9ff-1222-5097-80a1-d2c8e3522b4a" :answer/id #uuid "0495d9ff-1222-5097-80a1-d2c8e3522b4a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71026400-c6fb-5c50-8a9e-8626cef1e343" :answer/id #uuid "71026400-c6fb-5c50-8a9e-8626cef1e343" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d43a0d1-25dc-5b09-b9c3-641cd88bdb66" :answer/id #uuid "2d43a0d1-25dc-5b09-b9c3-641cd88bdb66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e88bf6aa-56d0-5447-b3d1-8bec7e8eb9d1" :question/id #uuid "e88bf6aa-56d0-5447-b3d1-8bec7e8eb9d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-99289" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=5{x}^{7},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(3{x}^{2}\\cdot 5{x}^{7}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }((3\\cdot 5){x}^{2+7}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(15\\cdot {x}^{9}) \\\\  & =15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{9}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $15$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{9}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(15{x}^{9})=-\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-785495d1-b945-5b35-848c-1112df719677"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-785495d1-b945-5b35-848c-1112df719677" :answer-field/id #uuid "785495d1-b945-5b35-848c-1112df719677" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f9936a72-9fad-5b49-893b-5a1b6c454bd1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a8ae8bd4-10e7-5e5c-99f9-4901d6b82be4" "answer-f9936a72-9fad-5b49-893b-5a1b6c454bd1" "answer-d6bda25e-5bda-5994-87a1-09a475eb1182" "answer-0d27e1a7-6910-59ed-bd3d-2ff6d6328c02" "answer-b8349b7a-9285-555f-9276-3a5603cf4fad"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8ae8bd4-10e7-5e5c-99f9-4901d6b82be4" :answer/id #uuid "a8ae8bd4-10e7-5e5c-99f9-4901d6b82be4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9936a72-9fad-5b49-893b-5a1b6c454bd1" :answer/id #uuid "f9936a72-9fad-5b49-893b-5a1b6c454bd1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6bda25e-5bda-5994-87a1-09a475eb1182" :answer/id #uuid "d6bda25e-5bda-5994-87a1-09a475eb1182" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d27e1a7-6910-59ed-bd3d-2ff6d6328c02" :answer/id #uuid "0d27e1a7-6910-59ed-bd3d-2ff6d6328c02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8349b7a-9285-555f-9276-3a5603cf4fad" :answer/id #uuid "b8349b7a-9285-555f-9276-3a5603cf4fad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b2f7fefa-e39c-5728-9247-538da8dbc2c9" :question/id #uuid "b2f7fefa-e39c-5728-9247-538da8dbc2c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-99294" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=6{x}^{9}$ and $q(x)=15{x}^{7},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)}{p(x)}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot (15{x}^{7})=15{x}^{9}\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)$ and $p(x),$ we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot q(x)}{p(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15{x}^{9}}{6{x}^{9}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{15}{6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ee8ef262-a304-54cc-b9b6-e3e50ca4de07"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ee8ef262-a304-54cc-b9b6-e3e50ca4de07" :answer-field/id #uuid "ee8ef262-a304-54cc-b9b6-e3e50ca4de07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d0a69e0a-a9c5-5723-a6b9-51c36e031c89" :answer-field/choices ["answer-75cc82e2-0552-5e05-afa6-010934ed1ebd" "answer-3987b6a8-c74c-528b-9cc2-085124cd1b92" "answer-d0a69e0a-a9c5-5723-a6b9-51c36e031c89" "answer-c246e2da-00be-5471-a59a-83ec193748b9" "answer-5eab2f79-eb6b-55d8-8015-08218199e422"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75cc82e2-0552-5e05-afa6-010934ed1ebd" :answer/id #uuid "75cc82e2-0552-5e05-afa6-010934ed1ebd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3987b6a8-c74c-528b-9cc2-085124cd1b92" :answer/id #uuid "3987b6a8-c74c-528b-9cc2-085124cd1b92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0a69e0a-a9c5-5723-a6b9-51c36e031c89" :answer/id #uuid "d0a69e0a-a9c5-5723-a6b9-51c36e031c89" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c246e2da-00be-5471-a59a-83ec193748b9" :answer/id #uuid "c246e2da-00be-5471-a59a-83ec193748b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5eab2f79-eb6b-55d8-8015-08218199e422" :answer/id #uuid "5eab2f79-eb6b-55d8-8015-08218199e422" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "DNE" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-be0311ba-56bf-5adc-9620-b550fa934c05" :question/id #uuid "be0311ba-56bf-5adc-9620-b550fa934c05" :question/math-academy-id "q-336941" :question/problem "If $u(x)=14{x}^{11}$ and $v(x)=-7{x}^{11},$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}=$  \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "When we divide the two functions, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{14{x}^{11}}{(-7{x}^{11})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-2) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3ba131f5-3a7e-5441-a963-a775a6a778f8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3ba131f5-3a7e-5441-a963-a775a6a778f8" :answer-field/id #uuid "3ba131f5-3a7e-5441-a963-a775a6a778f8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-dbc3a515-ec82-55c9-851c-75e95ba6146e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dbc3a515-ec82-55c9-851c-75e95ba6146e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dbc3a515-ec82-55c9-851c-75e95ba6146e" :answer/id #uuid "dbc3a515-ec82-55c9-851c-75e95ba6146e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186311146 :answer-field/correct "answer-2d260467-07cb-5e14-b0cb-436d603f9f4c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2d260467-07cb-5e14-b0cb-436d603f9f4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d260467-07cb-5e14-b0cb-436d603f9f4c" :answer/id #uuid "2d260467-07cb-5e14-b0cb-436d603f9f4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105950 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129160 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7a911604-8f85-54da-89bb-d261f841ad68"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128978 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1ccd9a2d6dacbb3651b5dc54121bd6aff3e1846d01c5e4855ea97c932f0f5fe.svg)\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/86/8668443119fd2ed6fd6994fc9d70a91a33c58e905e1efe6f7fcace1d62958883.svg)\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n![](images/e5/e5138ea7a633b391f344122e68170e9b0b6bad37b44e6d77be9fa5dc9ce65309.svg)\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_{1,2}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\color{red}\\lambda_3=-3}.$\n\n- From our matrix \n![](images/35/353e6de2f065c81002ec01bc4edd52c7e93b49f2a610800e1ec3be75f581c1e0.svg)\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n![](images/24/24f3190c7edbe900679dc69c5c413975c82015db74f9146aae64b017da1b0d4a.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129137 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-23d7ee0f-6a20-52e8-816d-b7bb5614c4c0" "question-f2511236-69db-51c8-aebc-e7ad77137885" "question-4d7eea0a-71f8-53db-9271-7bd029c6fb1c" "question-1b4b44b9-ba7c-5f44-b9ab-fefd02a1f1b2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128948 :question/problem "![](images/75/750197747be9a845e1db31b969d333edbbebf7a28ce85bcb61752ff29a872f83.svg)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2 \\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PBP^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/ab/abd031651857b6c66be090b1f3dc9cdeaff19ea1adac50dbfe234f0414f6afb1.svg)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-1(-1)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n$\\lambda_1={\\color{red}-1}.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 5}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n![](images/b0/b0fe98cefcd9a911ba02684ff93696bc14f468729d73d07055a7a1638c44f4ef.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129138 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fbaefedd-28e7-5c12-a8d5-d4c5ca3fba32"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128949 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n![](images/56/566569dbf0d6373b8482b41bc6fbc8b69d77eaf0cf5987151fe3854c77de9219.svg)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=1-\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/7e/7e33a1ba83b16d1b5767ee773aaa5d797eaab72949e98bb51aed6fbd249ab386.svg)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](images/22/22f1869f42a464e5d682ae6e7b5b33c75b8a1d480869baa5fc036ffaaa4d8417.svg)\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} .$ Therefore, \n\n![](images/b1/b1f00ea903f35490283b270c21eb49e4934235a2173a0d3b25c8feac6a6a509d.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129139 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0d0837bb-a312-5fc1-a5fe-74a16c2207cb" "question-d78d725a-0502-5fe2-897f-ae0775056dbc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128950 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2 \\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n![](images/68/68d39dc9fbe16467e2039054956fafa2818ba7978262472c34aa01eab12330dc.svg)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=7 + 4\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_3=-3 + 2\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find a possible matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}=\\overline{\\color{red}\\lambda}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\lambda_3}=\\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} = \\overline{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}4},$ \n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](images/e2/e217aa741bc60bcbacc8be44916b8c98a5f2433a2473bdb308467a032fcdc5d8.svg)\nand\n![](images/66/66248fe00b7c6c8b246cf434e181cb6eb8377df791d19e2031760414f01a54f1.svg)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](images/b1/b170bd9280cab68d887b417f5eb3b7ff6d61f78094939ab76f9e7c98bb43f4cc.svg)\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}.$ Therefore,\n![](images/6f/6f5efdb321ab6b4f1e55b75d03262c7b4437fcbe2de7af853ba7cf562877efe5.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-7a911604-8f85-54da-89bb-d261f841ad68" :question/id #uuid "7a911604-8f85-54da-89bb-d261f841ad68" :question/math-academy-id "q-38917" :question/problem "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\n\n- From our matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n\n$$\n{v}_{1,2}=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-009ce921-bb1e-52ba-8b94-ddf423054ad9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-009ce921-bb1e-52ba-8b94-ddf423054ad9" :answer-field/id #uuid "009ce921-bb1e-52ba-8b94-ddf423054ad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ffd6bce9-f18f-53b1-9912-62a627ae6f1d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-317b66ea-a2ed-5b63-b2c7-67056caf8cdd" "answer-ffd6bce9-f18f-53b1-9912-62a627ae6f1d" "answer-e8aff0e2-9d62-516e-8d28-e5fb7fafa6c6" "answer-d197643c-d92d-5245-83d0-3b43e501d4a9" "answer-38d5a319-fe62-57c5-9d19-50dbddcdfa78"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-317b66ea-a2ed-5b63-b2c7-67056caf8cdd" :answer/id #uuid "317b66ea-a2ed-5b63-b2c7-67056caf8cdd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 8\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffd6bce9-f18f-53b1-9912-62a627ae6f1d" :answer/id #uuid "ffd6bce9-f18f-53b1-9912-62a627ae6f1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8aff0e2-9d62-516e-8d28-e5fb7fafa6c6" :answer/id #uuid "e8aff0e2-9d62-516e-8d28-e5fb7fafa6c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =-8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}5\\pm 6\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 6\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d197643c-d92d-5245-83d0-3b43e501d4a9" :answer/id #uuid "d197643c-d92d-5245-83d0-3b43e501d4a9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =7\\pm 4\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ \\pm \\text{i} \\\\ 4\\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38d5a319-fe62-57c5-9d19-50dbddcdfa78" :answer/id #uuid "38d5a319-fe62-57c5-9d19-50dbddcdfa78" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$\\lambda =8\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\pm 6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-23d7ee0f-6a20-52e8-816d-b7bb5614c4c0" :question/id #uuid "23d7ee0f-6a20-52e8-816d-b7bb5614c4c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-135471" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 8 & 3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((6-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-2(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-8\\lambda +20) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=3.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{(-8)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 20}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pm 4\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3c0d5940-d65f-514a-a8b5-908d7590f27c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3c0d5940-d65f-514a-a8b5-908d7590f27c" :answer-field/id #uuid "3c0d5940-d65f-514a-a8b5-908d7590f27c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4cb9a1ee-6610-50e1-b406-85c2eb0e6005" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ae6f3612-8594-59c7-81c1-353ddb745f20" "answer-1fcd3fbe-3465-52fa-91b0-ecdf6eef743f" "answer-4cb9a1ee-6610-50e1-b406-85c2eb0e6005" "answer-e39973e4-45f0-57c4-9b9f-8fcf7ec25b82" "answer-29a4820b-50ed-55fd-9d58-4418607b4cb3"] :db/ensure [:answer-