h corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-7-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ -4 & 0 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )((-7-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )-5(-4)) & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +41) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-2.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{(10)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 41}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-68f8092c-7c2b-5d58-ae67-c2f1ccabc279"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-68f8092c-7c2b-5d58-ae67-c2f1ccabc279" :answer-field/id #uuid "68f8092c-7c2b-5d58-ae67-c2f1ccabc279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-444d2ba3-d5e0-51cc-a7bf-2e59c0f7e046" :answer-field/choices ["answer-444d2ba3-d5e0-51cc-a7bf-2e59c0f7e046" "answer-cda8ecc1-0eed-5b98-9c26-6689db877867" "answer-433b5384-8a47-5bf4-8248-feeb545bb5bd" "answer-c82b1b59-046b-5762-bdef-3f6575ddb33c" "answer-32bb690a-1edd-598a-8f92-be2849b7ba3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-444d2ba3-d5e0-51cc-a7bf-2e59c0f7e046" :answer/id #uuid "444d2ba3-d5e0-51cc-a7bf-2e59c0f7e046" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cda8ecc1-0eed-5b98-9c26-6689db877867" :answer/id #uuid "cda8ecc1-0eed-5b98-9c26-6689db877867" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-433b5384-8a47-5bf4-8248-feeb545bb5bd" :answer/id #uuid "433b5384-8a47-5bf4-8248-feeb545bb5bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c82b1b59-046b-5762-bdef-3f6575ddb33c" :answer/id #uuid "c82b1b59-046b-5762-bdef-3f6575ddb33c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32bb690a-1edd-598a-8f92-be2849b7ba3b" :answer/id #uuid "32bb690a-1edd-598a-8f92-be2849b7ba3b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d7eea0a-71f8-53db-9271-7bd029c6fb1c" :question/id #uuid "4d7eea0a-71f8-53db-9271-7bd029c6fb1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38914" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & -13 \\\\ -6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}4-\\lambda  & 0 & -13 \\\\ -6 & -7-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-7-\\lambda )((4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-2(-13)) & =0 \\\\ (-7-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda +34) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-7.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 34}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-82120635-48b6-5824-9056-6cc6780ef576"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-82120635-48b6-5824-9056-6cc6780ef576" :answer-field/id #uuid "82120635-48b6-5824-9056-6cc6780ef576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-832998ab-6ec8-5acf-9997-065a0a77d05e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-832998ab-6ec8-5acf-9997-065a0a77d05e" "answer-b59a4ba6-e327-5893-a6cf-6dfe76e8deee" "answer-624e25ce-a5a6-560c-b6eb-07d2c1b44e12" "answer-db8cac43-3f1f-5d0d-ace1-7184fee5b703"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-832998ab-6ec8-5acf-9997-065a0a77d05e" :answer/id #uuid "832998ab-6ec8-5acf-9997-065a0a77d05e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b59a4ba6-e327-5893-a6cf-6dfe76e8deee" :answer/id #uuid "b59a4ba6-e327-5893-a6cf-6dfe76e8deee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-624e25ce-a5a6-560c-b6eb-07d2c1b44e12" :answer/id #uuid "624e25ce-a5a6-560c-b6eb-07d2c1b44e12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db8cac43-3f1f-5d0d-ace1-7184fee5b703" :answer/id #uuid "db8cac43-3f1f-5d0d-ace1-7184fee5b703" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1b4b44b9-ba7c-5f44-b9ab-fefd02a1f1b2" :question/id #uuid "1b4b44b9-ba7c-5f44-b9ab-fefd02a1f1b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-135519" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -4-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )^{2}-5(-5)) & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +26) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-4.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 26}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0adbc0e4-187e-56fd-8b95-21d149ac375f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0adbc0e4-187e-56fd-8b95-21d149ac375f" :answer-field/id #uuid "0adbc0e4-187e-56fd-8b95-21d149ac375f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-91275c69-8063-5cb2-b49c-515df48585f4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-47b27817-bd80-505f-b07b-c3c158425c85" "answer-cf53e7fc-c0b1-596c-b202-7fc49f68a694" "answer-91275c69-8063-5cb2-b49c-515df48585f4" "answer-f8908321-ea0c-5285-b60a-63e814cafb5e" "answer-09b68aea-97d5-51b6-9132-7df35f600448"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-47b27817-bd80-505f-b07b-c3c158425c85" :answer/id #uuid "47b27817-bd80-505f-b07b-c3c158425c85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cf53e7fc-c0b1-596c-b202-7fc49f68a694" :answer/id #uuid "cf53e7fc-c0b1-596c-b202-7fc49f68a694" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-91275c69-8063-5cb2-b49c-515df48585f4" :answer/id #uuid "91275c69-8063-5cb2-b49c-515df48585f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8908321-ea0c-5285-b60a-63e814cafb5e" :answer/id #uuid "f8908321-ea0c-5285-b60a-63e814cafb5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09b68aea-97d5-51b6-9132-7df35f600448" :answer/id #uuid "09b68aea-97d5-51b6-9132-7df35f600448" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fbaefedd-28e7-5c12-a8d5-d4c5ca3fba32" :question/id #uuid "fbaefedd-28e7-5c12-a8d5-d4c5ca3fba32" :question/math-academy-id "q-38885" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ 1+4\\text{i} \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3+5\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1d98f8e9-9d6e-5d2c-b15c-295f14d86b6e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1d98f8e9-9d6e-5d2c-b15c-295f14d86b6e" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d98f8e9-9d6e-5d2c-b15c-295f14d86b6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2df44d7b-3fe5-59c3-a32d-b054a9cc4efb" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7480a3aa-70a0-5247-9a31-5c1c0daba1af" "answer-2df44d7b-3fe5-59c3-a32d-b054a9cc4efb" "answer-627e06b9-a4f1-547f-b90c-619445bcbf56" "answer-58cb07a3-f5b3-5b99-8901-735dd9a5bbd2" "answer-035ceaee-b6ca-5bb4-bfb9-11a4ca0e530b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7480a3aa-70a0-5247-9a31-5c1c0daba1af" :answer/id #uuid "7480a3aa-70a0-5247-9a31-5c1c0daba1af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2df44d7b-3fe5-59c3-a32d-b054a9cc4efb" :answer/id #uuid "2df44d7b-3fe5-59c3-a32d-b054a9cc4efb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-627e06b9-a4f1-547f-b90c-619445bcbf56" :answer/id #uuid "627e06b9-a4f1-547f-b90c-619445bcbf56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58cb07a3-f5b3-5b99-8901-735dd9a5bbd2" :answer/id #uuid "58cb07a3-f5b3-5b99-8901-735dd9a5bbd2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-035ceaee-b6ca-5bb4-bfb9-11a4ca0e530b" :answer/id #uuid "035ceaee-b6ca-5bb4-bfb9-11a4ca0e530b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0d0837bb-a312-5fc1-a5fe-74a16c2207cb" :question/id #uuid "0d0837bb-a312-5fc1-a5fe-74a16c2207cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-135539" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1+4\\text{i} \\\\ -6+\\text{i} \\\\ 5+\\text{i} \\\\ 3-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1+5\\text{i} \\\\ -4-4\\text{i} \\\\ 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -5-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 1 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-caf88bf2-1584-5767-b02d-c2d3fa67af5c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-caf88bf2-1584-5767-b02d-c2d3fa67af5c" :answer-field/id #uuid "caf88bf2-1584-5767-b02d-c2d3fa67af5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9fd78c44-bda0-58d3-a7b9-8a8aaefd389a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9fd78c44-bda0-58d3-a7b9-8a8aaefd389a" "answer-40074340-d749-5f86-b10c-2825baa1aec2" "answer-66ac5e6e-8d56-56a0-9cbc-d8a60681de45" "answer-cfd0a51b-0c69-594b-a56c-77159dcf3138" "answer-c3355bb9-c2db-5224-ae74-770c5b30a759"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fd78c44-bda0-58d3-a7b9-8a8aaefd389a" :answer/id #uuid "9fd78c44-bda0-58d3-a7b9-8a8aaefd389a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 1 & 5 \\\\ -6 & 1 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-40074340-d749-5f86-b10c-2825baa1aec2" :answer/id #uuid "40074340-d749-5f86-b10c-2825baa1aec2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 1 & -4 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66ac5e6e-8d56-56a0-9cbc-d8a60681de45" :answer/id #uuid "66ac5e6e-8d56-56a0-9cbc-d8a60681de45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cfd0a51b-0c69-594b-a56c-77159dcf3138" :answer/id #uuid "cfd0a51b-0c69-594b-a56c-77159dcf3138" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 1 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3355bb9-c2db-5224-ae74-770c5b30a759" :answer/id #uuid "c3355bb9-c2db-5224-ae74-770c5b30a759" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -6 & -4 & -4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -5 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d78d725a-0502-5fe2-897f-ae0775056dbc" :question/id #uuid "d78d725a-0502-5fe2-897f-ae0775056dbc" :question/math-academy-id "q-135535" :question/problem "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2\\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-5-\\text{i} \\\\ -2+5\\text{i} \\\\ -4+3\\text{i} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}4+4\\text{i} \\\\ 1-5\\text{i} \\\\ 4-6\\text{i} \\\\ 1-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1-2\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-4-\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d10e5258-d2f4-58bf-af8d-2359214f5726"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d10e5258-d2f4-58bf-af8d-2359214f5726" :answer-field/id #uuid "d10e5258-d2f4-58bf-af8d-2359214f5726" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8669ee5b-2412-57d2-b0aa-06341f0d07e7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f4688075-facf-538c-bcae-94936c913af5" "answer-b71c6ea4-3aa2-5b7e-8818-b3735f517002" "answer-0b8bc467-4f82-5758-99ba-9799d92a2ddf" "answer-8669ee5b-2412-57d2-b0aa-06341f0d07e7" "answer-eb73eef6-715d-508e-a66b-4d05b04b5bf1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4688075-facf-538c-bcae-94936c913af5" :answer/id #uuid "f4688075-facf-538c-bcae-94936c913af5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -2 & 1 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b71c6ea4-3aa2-5b7e-8818-b3735f517002" :answer/id #uuid "b71c6ea4-3aa2-5b7e-8818-b3735f517002" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b8bc467-4f82-5758-99ba-9799d92a2ddf" :answer/id #uuid "0b8bc467-4f82-5758-99ba-9799d92a2ddf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8669ee5b-2412-57d2-b0aa-06341f0d07e7" :answer/id #uuid "8669ee5b-2412-57d2-b0aa-06341f0d07e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & -5 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb73eef6-715d-508e-a66b-4d05b04b5bf1" :answer/id #uuid "eb73eef6-715d-508e-a66b-4d05b04b5bf1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -1 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 4 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 5 & 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130356 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **block diagonalizable** if it is similar to a block diagonal matrix $B.$ That is, there exists a block diagonal matrix $B$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PBP^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFor example, the matrix \n\n![](images/18/1857eb7b5b094ae9cdb3c233be3513e1c9a725d68d104145db573f02be065340.svg) \n\nis block diagonalizable because it can be factored as follows:\n\n![](images/43/43f25f96d39e392d7b1b0910eee54650e6b3c1a977c0cc43c4c04aebe2f4b085.svg)\n\nHere, the matrix $A$ has eigenvalues \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\lambda_1 = 2},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2=3-2\\textrm i},\\qquad \n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_3=3+2\\textrm i}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\lambda_2 = \\overline{\\lambda_3}.$\nThe eigenvectors of ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}, {\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_3},$ respectively, are\n\n![](images/a9/a91e32ad98d0b2bf93053745873fd219c2ed6fbd533c3e8a974032d63feea161.svg)\n\nAgain, notice that $\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\overline{\\mathbf{v}_3}.$\nThe first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1},$ and the second and third columns correspond to the real and the imaginary parts of ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130357 :tutorial/content "We have the following theorem regarding the block diagonalization of a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix:\n\n*If $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then* \n\n$$\nA=PBP^{-1},\n$$\n\n*where the matrices $B$ and $P$ are given by*\n![](images/97/97adc950e68b3e7bce042b741e7b0a33f4baa2f9aae91f9bb40b4bbe36b0b595.svg)\n\nIn other words, if $A$ is a real $3\\!\\times\\! 3$ matrix with one real eigenvalue and two complex eigenvalues, then $A$ is block diagonalizable.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The block of $B$ constructed from the real and imaginary parts of $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- We can write the blocks of $B$ in either order. However, the order of the columns of $P$ *must* correspond to the order of the blocks." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129362 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054722 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c5c3534c-ace7-5657-a9dc-80aa55f51084" "question-7984db9b-dab8-5221-ba35-8d0a5e549cba"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c5c3534c-ace7-5657-a9dc-80aa55f51084" :question/id #uuid "c5c3534c-ace7-5657-a9dc-80aa55f51084" :question/math-academy-id "q-23981" :question/problem "Which of the following shows the graph of $y={x}^{2}-3x+5?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's construct a table of values for the function $y={x}^{2}-3x+5.$ We fill the first row with the $x$-values $x=-1,0,1,2,3,4$ and then use the formula $y={x}^{2}-3x+5$ to compute the corresponding $y$-values in the second row.\n\n| $\\,\\,x\\,\\,$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,\\,y\\,\\,$ | $9$ | $5$ | $3$ | $3$ | $5$ | $9$ |\n\nIf we plot those points on a graph, we can start to see the shape of a parabola:\n\n![](images/5a/5a7374c0e81f36d4d320abf38dcf72ac0b377c335894c601bfae82b64ea0bca3.png)\n\nWe fill out the graph by drawing the parabola through the plotted points:\n\n![](images/f6/f671cee2e37f7905c220357782d2d4f2e6361d4858030084492e216d3f3031cd.png)" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-636c8023-db68-55b4-ae54-78725778fd9e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-636c8023-db68-55b4-ae54-78725778fd9e" :answer-field/id #uuid "636c8023-db68-55b4-ae54-78725778fd9e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-63e31f21-22da-5f21-b0c2-d0c9a0ddaefa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a6e97a60-753f-5d6a-af1d-3fcdedc3559c" "answer-42e07ce2-bc8b-58a5-9504-4e7eb9582a3f" "answer-a94ecbb5-eea4-59d0-b204-e4dcc12c3179" "answer-b71126cb-562d-5bc2-bef7-a0e8477bbcdb" "answer-63e31f21-22da-5f21-b0c2-d0c9a0ddaefa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6e97a60-753f-5d6a-af1d-3fcdedc3559c" :answer/id #uuid "a6e97a60-753f-5d6a-af1d-3fcdedc3559c" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/07/0779906575c293797e031d96b507cdcbd5f6380dcd497ea2885c059da07d6da7.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-42e07ce2-bc8b-58a5-9504-4e7eb9582a3f" :answer/id #uuid "42e07ce2-bc8b-58a5-9504-4e7eb9582a3f" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/6a/6ab5e5cdef936707ab8e9bd7fdc54c790ff4ce93cd3ef8af44aae44e8cd77764.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a94ecbb5-eea4-59d0-b204-e4dcc12c3179" :answer/id #uuid "a94ecbb5-eea4-59d0-b204-e4dcc12c3179" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/8f/8fcd77fa59730639e108526ab31c73086db42e744353ff57a64f4df96c39a116.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b71126cb-562d-5bc2-bef7-a0e8477bbcdb" :answer/id #uuid "b71126cb-562d-5bc2-bef7-a0e8477bbcdb" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/b8/b868ef7ef919776ed69ceea87aefaa46787794353e5b390c27368fe5dd66b972.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-63e31f21-22da-5f21-b0c2-d0c9a0ddaefa" :answer/id #uuid "63e31f21-22da-5f21-b0c2-d0c9a0ddaefa" :answer/type :answer.type/image :answer/value "images/f6/f671cee2e37f7905c220357782d2d4f2e6361d4858030084492e216d3f3031cd.png" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054721 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3d69fa39-1eb4-5e90-8609-317c0513eaff" "question-01c1b80a-b0c5-5bb0-af0e-20dd6de4f28c"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3d69fa39-1eb4-5e90-8609-317c0513eaff" :question/id #uuid "3d69fa39-1eb4-5e90-8609-317c0513eaff" :question/math-academy-id "q-24637" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e5c26cd78c9f3e778f5396b2cfda357042ddacc467783cb657ed9d5364665c4.png)\n\nFind the roots of the parabola shown above." :question/worked-solution "The roots of a parabola are the $x$-intercepts of the parabola.\n\nFrom the graph, we can see that the curve does not intercept the $x$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the parabola does not have any roots." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88ac794d-2b1b-52d1-873f-37b7753f77b6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88ac794d-2b1b-52d1-873f-37b7753f77b6" :answer-field/id #uuid "88ac794d-2b1b-52d1-873f-37b7753f77b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-104e04eb-0268-563b-88fc-22075c061db8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0cdb967f-21c2-5170-b53d-16d5028e4ed0" "answer-96957528-289f-58b7-a316-2d142247431e" "answer-756eb032-8a5b-5360-9dc8-c4731f589bca" "answer-a197812f-198c-576c-b3c5-5d39d4ceb48c" "answer-104e04eb-0268-563b-88fc-22075c061db8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0cdb967f-21c2-5170-b53d-16d5028e4ed0" :answer/id #uuid "0cdb967f-21c2-5170-b53d-16d5028e4ed0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-96957528-289f-58b7-a316-2d142247431e" :answer/id #uuid "96957528-289f-58b7-a316-2d142247431e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-756eb032-8a5b-5360-9dc8-c4731f589bca" :answer/id #uuid "756eb032-8a5b-5360-9dc8-c4731f589bca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=8$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a197812f-198c-576c-b3c5-5d39d4ceb48c" :answer/id #uuid "a197812f-198c-576c-b3c5-5d39d4ceb48c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=-8$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-104e04eb-0268-563b-88fc-22075c061db8" :answer/id #uuid "104e04eb-0268-563b-88fc-22075c061db8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No roots" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054685 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dcd95f6f-d031-5291-b596-6c28f4bcefd5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dcd95f6f-d031-5291-b596-6c28f4bcefd5" :question/id #uuid "dcd95f6f-d031-5291-b596-6c28f4bcefd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-83559" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ecd951a63d500d0bf6b5234a65b8df55ada27882be337df6aea7d7aaa516e91b.png)\n\nThe equation of the curve above is $y=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}x+k.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we substitute $x=0$ into the given equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}x+k \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{4}(0)^{2}-\\frac{3}{4}(0)+k \\\\ y & =k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nmeaning that the curve intercepts the $y$-axis at the point $(0,k).$ Based on the graph, we can find that $k=\\frac{1}{2}$ since our parabola intercepts the $y$-axis at \n$(0,\\frac{1}{2}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ce7f934-30bb-5661-a72f-65673ac7cfc6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ce7f934-30bb-5661-a72f-65673ac7cfc6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ce7f934-30bb-5661-a72f-65673ac7cfc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9d110e39-d8dd-5043-9919-f6ab4ffa4686" :answer-field/choice